  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "The diagonal run frees the third player",
  "description": "Five against five in an area of about 40 × 35 metres. At one end is a goal with a goalkeeper, at the other end two cone goals about five metres wide out by the sidelines. One team attacks the big goal, the other scores by dribbling the ball through a cone goal. Switch ends halfway through.\n\nThe team attacking the big goal should make lots of diagonal runs, long ones too – from the wing in towards the middle or from the middle out towards the wing. The ball carrier has three choices: play the runner, dummy the pass and dribble, or use the space the run has left and find a third player. A goal where a diagonal run was part of the attack earns an extra point.\n\nVariation: play with offside now and then, so the players have to time the diagonal run along the back line. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Run diagonally – then the defender has to choose between following you and letting you go."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Dare to make long runs even when you don't get the ball; they create space for others."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "On the ball: read what the defenders do when the run starts, and choose from that."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Fill the space the runner leaves – that is where the third player is free."
    }
  ],
